### Describe the problem

Memory leaks is a tough problem in a large interactive SPA. It's hard to notice on time and sometimes even harder to diagnose. Here I want to propose how Svelte might be a bit better citizen by minimizing impact of some memory leaks.

Check [this REPL](https://svelte.dev/playground/hello-world?version=5.55.3#H4sIAAAAAAAAE3VSy26DMBD8la1TKSBFcXqlBCm3HnrovVSVA0tjxdjIXkoixL_XGJo2fZzMzu7M2LP0TIsaWcIeUCkDnbGqhAhLSVjGbMUqqdCx5LlndG7GuRHw-MzaNc3avaOiEdsLh3_hhdGEmrwMS11hZUNZrnOSdWMswSOKI1TW1LBc87GYiUs_46cUEriD6WALt44EYVQJ5TC-z3XKv9R0um-JjAajCyWL47aPYthmEM3cm_GMhyw0gTpZIJAB5f1SPlGDTL-QVfAbRvO0VeGqOfULFMUBdtaKc3S32cRrH4SKNjEIB68rkLrEE0ThiIeJ4_lKZp_fvgpP5Rck5Zd2z0f5yZMH057Lytc-PsITsYRsi8PqnzV8i-16D9eN34uA2pStwnAL33UEjd-EdOgj09jB01RFU5j9EP9M3TUiBOcDEp2QF_4cgTKilPotDCR0QD3jpSAxgYWg-eE5obXGBpgHsSG4TQ7XQbz4SkjV-bxZEn6I4QODj-94ygIAAA).

Because promise is never resolved, every toggle to the button will lead to a lot of detached DOM nodes leaking. Now I do understand that the developer is fully at fault here, but I think Svelte could minimize an impact of such a mistake by not causing DOM subtree to leak.

The example here is of course intentionally simple. In our real world application we have a huge subtrees. Because the nature of our application is such that tab is long living and user clicks toggles through subtrees a lot, this can result in a lot of memory leaking fast (I am talking gigabytes of RAM). However if we remove detached nodes leak, then impact won't be probably that noticeable.

Ideally we find this leak and fix it. But whenever you have many developers working on a large codebase it's not always possible. At the same time users of our software do not need to pay that big of a price for our mistakes :)

### Describe the proposed solution

Following is sort of a pseudo code, I didn't test the idea, just want to outline it.

Currently `Leak` component will compile to something like this
```js
const promise = new Promise(() => {});

export default function Leak($$anchor, $$props) {
	$.await(
		node,
		() => promise,
		($$anchor) => {},
		($$anchor) => {},
		($$anchor) => {}
	);
}
```

but what if we do this instead
```js
const promise = new Promise(() => {});

export default function Leak($$anchor, $$props) {
	var ctrl = new AbortController()
	var abort = new Promise((_, reject) => ctrl.signal.addEventListener('abort', reject))

	$.await(
		node,
		() => Promise.race(promise, abort),
		($$anchor) => {},
		($$anchor) => {},
		($$anchor) => {}
	);

	onDestroy(() => {
	  ctrl.abort();
	})
}
```

This way at least the whole subtree won't leak with the promise.

### Importance

would make my life easier

