During hydration, `set_attribute` compares the server attribute (a string from `getAttribute()`) with the client value. When the value is a number or boolean, the two never compare equal, so attributes like `tabindex={-1}`, `aria-rowindex={i}`, `colspan={1}`, `<svg width={24} stroke-width={1.5}>` or `data-x={false}` are set again with the string they already have.

If the page observes the DOM, each rewrite enqueues an attribute mutation record, and it can add style and layout work. In a 29-row table with a synthetic mix of attributes, Chromium records 493 attribute mutations during hydration on `main`, although the markup already matches. 290 of them are eligible to be skipped: plain `setAttribute` writes of numbers and booleans that store exactly the string already there. The other 203 are preserved: they go through property setters (IDL setters such as canvas dimensions, spread `disabled`/`required`, and compiled property assignments), which can have side effects even when they store an identical string. The candidate removes 290 attribute mutation records in this fixture while retaining 203 writes.

`set_class` already skips unchanged values during hydration. I intend to submit a PR that, during hydration, skips only `setAttribute` calls that would store the exact string the server rendered for a number or boolean. Property setters keep running, and so do writes to URL attributes such as an SVG `<image href>`, because writing the same URL again can fire another `load` or `error` event, and to SVG list attributes such as `<text x>`, because writing one detaches list items retrieved through `baseVal`. Writes to autonomous custom elements are kept, and so are writes to upgraded customized built-ins, which are recognized by their prototype, so also after `is` is removed. One limitation: an upgraded customized built-in whose prototype was replaced with the native one is compared as native, so it gets no `attributeChangedCallback` for these skipped values. That is how `main` already treats unchanged strings on any customized built-in.

