If a user picks an option in a server-rendered `<select>` before hydration has finished (slow network, large bundle), Svelte 5 resets the select to the server value while it hydrates. The user's choice is silently lost, `onchange` never fires, and state stays at the old value.

Affected:
- `<select bind:value>`
- `<select {value} onchange={...}>` (one-way)

`<input bind:value>` already handles this: `bind_value` in `packages/svelte/src/internal/client/dom/elements/bindings/input.js` checks `hydrating && input.defaultValue !== input.value` and adopts the DOM value (from #11986 / #12083, see #12050). `<select>` has no such guard:

- `bind_select_value` in `bindings/select.js` only takes the DOM selection when the bound value is `undefined` (`mounting && value === undefined`). Otherwise it calls `select_option(select, value, mounting)`, which overwrites what the user picked.
- one-way `{value}` compiles to `select.value = (select.__value = value) ?? ''; $.select_option(select, value)` in a template effect, which also overwrites the selection unconditionally.

Not affected (current workaround): no `value` on the `<select>`, with `selected={...}` on each `<option>` instead. The user's choice survives hydration, and later changes to `selected` still take effect.

Side note: one-way `<input {value}>` is reset the same way (via `set_value`), but `<input bind:value>` is not. This issue is mainly about `<select>`, where even `bind:value` loses the input.

For comparison, Vue fixed the equivalent problem for text inputs in vuejs/core#15210 (PR vuejs/core#15211).

### Expected behaviour

The same as `<input bind:value>`: a selection made while hydration is in progress should be adopted (bound state updated to the DOM selection) instead of being overwritten with the server value.

### Possible fix (sketch)

In `bind_select_value`, while `hydrating`, check whether the DOM selection differs from the server-rendered one (`option.selected !== option.defaultSelected` for any option, since SSR emits `selected` on the matching option). If it does, read the value from `:checked` and call `set(value)` instead of `select_option(...)`, analogous to `input.js`. The check needs to run synchronously in `bind_select_value` (while `hydrating` is still true), not inside the `effect`. The one-way path could use the same check before its first `select_option` call.


