### Describe the bug

On the server, when a component inside a `<svelte:boundary>` with a `failed` snippet throws synchronously, the `onDestroy` callbacks registered inside that boundary never run, both the throwing component's and those of siblings that rendered before it.

#18585 (fixing #18584) made server `onDestroy` callbacks run whether a render succeeds or throws, and the other failure paths now do. This one is still missed:

| Where the error is thrown | `onDestroy` of components that already ran |
| --- | --- |
| No boundary, sync or async | run ✅ |
| Boundary with `failed`, after an `await` | run ✅ |
| Boundary with `failed`, synchronously | **skipped** ❌ |

The cause appears to be the synchronous `catch` in `Renderer#boundary`, which clears the boundary's renderer before rendering `failed` into it ([`child.#out.length = 0`](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/707c28146b0f0a6d5404a1bd4769874c3c24851a/packages/svelte/src/internal/server/renderer.js#L376)). That drops the component renderers holding the callbacks, so `#collect_on_destroy` never reaches them. The async path in `#collect_content_async` renders `failed` into a new renderer and leaves the boundary's children in place, which is why their callbacks still run there.

(On the client, `onDestroy` is tied to mounting, so these components, which never mount, don't run it either. On the server, though, `onDestroy` is the per-request cleanup hook, and #18584 explains why skipping it on errors leaks.)

Why it matters: we hit this in a library whose components subscribe to a store shared across requests and release the subscription in `onDestroy`. After a boundary catches an error, the subscriptions stay, and the next request renders state left over from the failed one. For now we also release on `getAbortSignal()`'s abort, which does fire.

### Reproduction

`Child.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
	import { onDestroy } from 'svelte';
	let { name, log, fail = false } = $props();
	log.push(`setup ${name}`);
	onDestroy(() => log.push(`destroy ${name}`));
	if (fail) throw new Error('boom');
</script>

<p>{name}</p>
```

`App.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
	import Child from './Child.svelte';
	let { log } = $props();
</script>

<svelte:boundary>
	<Child name="sibling" {log} />
	<Child name="thrower" {log} fail />
	{#snippet failed()}<p>failed</p>{/snippet}
</svelte:boundary>
<Child name="outside" {log} />
```

```js
import { render } from 'svelte/server';
import App from './App.svelte';

const log = [];
render(App, { props: { log }, transformError: (e) => ({ message: String(e) }) }).body;
console.log(log);
```

Expected:

```
['setup sibling', 'setup thrower', 'setup outside', 'destroy sibling', 'destroy thrower', 'destroy outside']
```

Actual (the boundary's two `destroy` entries are missing):

```
['setup sibling', 'setup thrower', 'setup outside', 'destroy outside']
```

Making `thrower` throw after an `await` (with `experimental.async`) gives the expected log, and removing the boundary runs both callbacks before the render throws. The same happens in async mode when the throw is synchronous.

The playground can't pass `transformError` to the server render, so there's no REPL link. Here's the same thing as a test for `renderer.test.ts`, which fails on `main`:

```ts
test('on_destroy callbacks run when a sync render fails inside a boundary', () => {
	const destroyed: string[] = [];
	const component = (renderer: Renderer) => {
		renderer.boundary({ failed: (renderer: Renderer) => renderer.push('failed') }, (renderer) => {
			renderer.component((renderer) => {
				renderer.on_destroy(() => destroyed.push('a'));
				throw new Error('boom');
			});
		});
	};

	const { body } = Renderer.render(component as unknown as Component, {
		transformError: (error: unknown) => ({ message: String(error) })
	} as any);
	expect(body).toContain('failed');
	expect(destroyed).toEqual(['a']); // actual: []
});
```

