body_text:
### Describe the bug

With `experimental.async`, while one change is waiting on an `await` and another change is processed, so `batch_values` is set, a dirty `$derived` executes again on every read instead of once. A derived read N times runs N times, and one read inside another derived multiplies. In the reproduction below, `parsed` runs 2,000 times for one click while the DOM ends up correct. teemingc's comment below shows that through a chain of deriveds the re-runs compound and can freeze the tab.

The mechanism has come up before. [This comment on #18624](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/issues/18624#issuecomment-5355755057) describes it with SvelteKit's `Query.set()` as the trigger, and #18355 is about the same retained dirtiness during overlapping batches, there as an infinite loop. Two things here are new: the trigger is plain Svelte (one pending `await` plus an unrelated state change), and the amplification grew sharply in 5.46.3.

- Up to 5.46.1, `get()` didn't re-evaluate a derived when `batch_values.has(derived)`. Some redundant work already existed: `cells` runs 11 times in the reproduction, once per read plus one. #17362 (5.46.3) removed that condition. Now `get()` runs `is_dirty(derived)` and then `update_derived(derived)` ([runtime.js#L683-L692](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/86eb09b76ef9c81e8b34adf7eaaf63974a66aaa9/packages/svelte/src/internal/client/runtime.js#L683-L692)) before it consults `batch_values` ([#L700](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/86eb09b76ef9c81e8b34adf7eaaf63974a66aaa9/packages/svelte/src/internal/client/runtime.js#L700)).
- While `batch_values !== null`, `update_derived` caches the value in `batch_values` but deliberately leaves the derived's status dirty ([deriveds.js#L428-L436](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/86eb09b76ef9c81e8b34adf7eaaf63974a66aaa9/packages/svelte/src/internal/client/reactivity/deriveds.js#L428-L436)), so every read executes it again.
- A `MAYBE_DIRTY` chain does the same: `is_dirty` calls `update_derived(dependency)` ([runtime.js#L167-L169](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/86eb09b76ef9c81e8b34adf7eaaf63974a66aaa9/packages/svelte/src/internal/client/runtime.js#L167-L169)) and doesn't reset the status while time-travelling ([#L176-L183](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/blob/86eb09b76ef9c81e8b34adf7eaaf63974a66aaa9/packages/svelte/src/internal/client/runtime.js#L176-L183)).

Restoring only the `batch_values.has` condition brings `parsed` back to 1 but still runs `cells` 11 times, as in 5.46.1.

#18988 fixed both paths when tested at 8a1046e. It was merged into `async-another-try-pt-2`, not `main`, and that branch into #18880's, which is still open; the table below has `main` and #18880's head.

### Reproduction

<details>
<summary>Project setup (Vite 8.3.4, <code>@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte</code> 7.3.1, <code>experimental.async</code>)</summary>

```sh
npm init -y
npm pkg set type=module
npm install -D --save-exact svelte@5.57.2 vite@8.3.4 @sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte@7.3.1
npx vite
```

```js
// vite.config.js
import { svelte } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';

export default {
	plugins: [svelte({ compilerOptions: { experimental: { async: true } } })]
};
```

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<div id="app"></div>
<script type="module" src="/src/main.js"></script>
```

```js
// src/main.js
import { mount } from 'svelte';
import App from './App.svelte';

mount(App, { target: document.getElementById('app') });
```

</details>

`src/App.svelte`, where every promise is resolved by hand:

```svelte
<script>
	const pending = [];
	function load(week) {
		const { promise, resolve } = Promise.withResolvers();
		pending.push(() => resolve(week));
		return promise;
	}
	window.settle = () => pending.splice(0).forEach((resolve) => resolve());
	window.settleFirst = () => pending.shift()?.();

	const runs = (window.runs = { parsed: 0, cells: 0 });

	let week = $state(0);
