### Describe the bug

With `experimental.async`, removing one item from a hydrated keyed `{#each}` also removes every item rendered before it. Removing the last item empties the whole list. The state is correct; only the DOM is wrong.

It needs all of these:

1. the component was server-rendered and hydrated (a client-only `mount` is fine);
2. it has a top-level `await`;
3. a later top-level statement calls a function, so the compiler treats bindings it reaches as blocked on that `await`;
4. each item's sole child is a component whose props use that function, so every item renders inside its own `$.async(...)` block.

Drop any one of them (the `await`, the later call, the call in the template, or the component as the item's root) and the list behaves.

It's a regression in **5.56.2**: 5.56.1 is fine, and 5.56.2 through 5.57.2 are broken. 5.56.2 contains #18371 ("ensure async block assigns correct nodes to effect"), which changed how an `$.async` block that wraps a block's sole child assigns its end node during hydration. That fits the symptom: each item's effect seems to get an end node that reaches back over the earlier items. #18959 looks related (same sole-child `$.async` path, client remount instead of hydration).

### Reproduction

Gist: https://gist.github.com/maxschwarzmueller/6864811ba4c24824f076a498b9150007 (Vite SSR build, then hydration in Playwright; the playground can't hydrate).

`List.svelte`
```svelte
<script>
	import Item from './Item.svelte';

	let names = $state(await Promise.resolve(['a', 'b', 'c']));

	const label = (name) => name;
	const unused = label('x');
</script>

{#each names as name (name)}
	<Item title={label(name)}>
		{#snippet children()}
			<button aria-label="Remove {name}" onclick={() => (names = names.filter((n) => n !== name))}>x</button>
		{/snippet}
	</Item>
{/each}
```

`Item.svelte`
```svelte
<script>
	let { title, children } = $props();
</script>

<div class="row">
	<span>{title}</span>
	{@render children()}
</div>
```

The compiled client output wraps each item in its own `$.async` block:

```js
$.each(node, 16, () => $.get(names), (name) => name, ($$anchor, name) => {
	{
		$.async($$anchor, [$$promises[1]], void 0, ($$anchor) => {
			// ...
			Item($$anchor, { get title() { return $.get($0); }, children, $$slots: { default: true } });
		});
		$.next();
	}
});
```

Steps (`npm install && npx playwright install chromium`):

1. `npm run repro`: renders `List` with `svelte/server`, hydrates it, clicks **Remove b**.
2. `npm run repro:mount`: the same with `mount` instead of `hydrate`.

Expected: `a` and `c` remain in both cases.

Actual: after hydration only `c` remains. Clicking **Remove c** instead empties the list, and **Remove a** works.

### Logs

```shell
$ npm run repro
hydrated before: [ 'Remove a', 'Remove b', 'Remove c' ]
hydrated after:  [ 'Remove c' ]

$ npm run repro:mount
mounted before: [ 'Remove a', 'Remove b', 'Remove c' ]
mounted after:  [ 'Remove a', 'Remove c' ]
```

No errors or warnings in the console.

### System Info

```shell
svelte 5.57.2 (also 5.56.2–5.57.1; 5.56.1 is fine)
@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte 7.3.1, vite 8.3.4
compilerOptions.experimental.async: true
Node 24.21.0, Chromium 153 (Playwright 1.63.0), Linux
```

### Severity

annoyance

We work around it by moving the `await` into a parent component and passing the resolved data to a child that has no top-level `await`. Found in a SvelteKit 3 app (a team members list blanked out after removing one member); reduced to this pure-Svelte repro with help from Claude.

