### Which project does this relate to?

Router (`@tanstack/react-router` / `@tanstack/router-core`, SSR with streaming hydration)

### Describe the bug

If the browser's location changes after the server-rendered document commits but before the client hydrates, `hydrate` renders the routes for the new location against the markup of the old one.

Concretely: open page A, client-navigate to page B, reload B, and press Back while B's client JS is still loading. The address bar shows A and the router reports A as loaded, but the screen still shows B's server markup, with A rendered as a second app shell below it. Nothing is logged. With React 19 and `hydrateRoot(document, ...)`, a mismatch between direct children of `<body>` is skipped rather than recovered by client rendering, so the duplicated shell stays.

The cause is in `hydrate` (`@tanstack/router-core/dist/esm/load-client.js`, 1.170.44): after `router.options.hydrate` resolves, it reads `router.history.location`, calls `router.updateLatestLocation()`, and runs `router.matchRoutes(location)` for the **current** location. The dehydrated payload (`window.$_TSR.router`) carries match ids but not the href the server rendered, so `hydrate` has nothing else to match against. When a candidate's id differs from the dehydrated match id, it takes the `pendingBoundary` path built for `ssr: false` routes and treats the old page's server markup as pending UI for the new route. Location changes after that read are already handled (the hydration handoff's `claim` compares `router.history.location` with what it read, and `Transitioner` calls `router.load()` on mount); the window before the read is the one that isn't.

Per the HTML spec, entries created with `pushState` share the document state of the entry they came from, so after A → (client nav) → B → reload, the A entry points at the reloaded document and a traversal to it fires `popstate` on that document without loading a new one. That is why this happens before any script has run.

### Complete minimal reproducer

No hosted reproducer yet. The steps below reproduce it in any SSR TanStack Router app whose hydration takes longer than about a second after the document commits (throttle the network in DevTools, or add a delay before `hydrateRoot`).

### Steps to Reproduce the Bug

1. Load route `/a` of an SSR TanStack Router app.
2. Click a `<Link>` to `/b` (client navigation).
3. Reload `/b`.
4. Press the browser's Back button before the client hydrates.
5. The URL is `/a` and `/a`'s data loads, but `/b`'s server markup stays on screen and `/a` renders below it.

### Expected behavior

Hydration matches the URL the server rendered, so the server markup hydrates as `/b`, and the router then navigates to the current location (`/a`) once hydration commits, the same way it already handles a location change after its read.

One way to get there: dehydrate the server's href (or the matched location) alongside the match ids, have `hydrate` match against it, and treat a different `router.history.location` as a navigation after hydration. A narrower option is to have the browser history report the document URL until hydration finishes (`createBrowserHistory`'s `parseLocation` could do this today, but the replay afterwards has to be wired by hand).

### Screenshots or Videos

_No response_

### Platform

- Router version: 1.170.44 (`@tanstack/react-router`), React 19.3
- Browsers: Chrome and Firefox (reproduced); Safari not tested
- OS: macOS

### Additional context

Our workaround wraps `router.options.hydrate` and reloads when the location no longer matches the document's URL. It compares two URLs the browser serialized (the navigation timing entry and `window.location`), so it avoids the false positives Remix hit when it compared the server's view of the pathname:

```ts
export function setupReloadOnLocationChangeBeforeHydration(router: AnyRouter): void {
  const originalHydrate = router.options.hydrate;
  router.options.hydrate = async function hydrateUnlessLocationChanged(dehydrated: never) {
    if (originalHydrate != null) {
      await originalHydrate(dehydrated);
    }
    const documentEntry = performance.getEntriesByType('navigation')[0];
    if (documentEntry == null) {
      return;
    }
    const documentUrl = new URL(documentEntry.name);
    if (
      documentUrl.pathname === window.location.pathname &&
      documentUrl.search === window.location.search
    ) {
      return;
    }
    window.location.reload();
    // Hydration stays suspended, with the server markup on screen, until the reload replaces the document.
    await Promise.withResolvers<never>().promise;
  };
}
```

It runs last among the `hydrate` steps; `hydrate` reads the location in the microtasks after `router.options.hydrate` resolves, so no `popstate` task can run between the check and that read. A reload is a blunt fix, which is why a router-level fix seems better.

Other SSR routers have hit the same race:

- Remix: https://github.com/remix-run/remix/issues/1757 (same diagnosis), fixed with a reload in https://github.com/remix-run/remix/pull/6409, later removed in https://github.com/remix-run/remix/pull/9890 because comparing the server's pathname with `window.location` looped on trailing slashes, encoded characters and 404s, and because React 18 recovered from the mismatch by client rendering.
- React Router 7: https://github.com/remix-run/react-router/issues/13873 (open), reload then instant Back gives "No result found for routeId" and hydration errors in production.
- Next.js: fixed in https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/96252 by hydrating the server's tree and replaying the missed traversal (detected with the Navigation API); its first version needed https://github.com/react/react/pull/37135.
- SvelteKit: https://github.com/sveltejs/kit/issues/10876 (open), with a reload as the workaround.

