body_text:
A few years ago, borderless windows on Windows 10 already had issues (see #11088). Unfortunately, the problem was never fully resolved and has evolved into a different manifestation.

When creating a window with `decorations = false` on Windows 10, the top border is missing:

<img width="654" height="436" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dca18d96-83a5-482c-865a-ac541509cd9a" />

This is the same issue that also exists in WinUI 3. Recently, the WinUI 3 team fixed it in the following PR:

[Fix a bug where using a custom TitleBar on Windows 10 results in the window's top border being drawn incorrectly](https://github.com/microsoft/microsoft-ui-xaml/pull/11698)

I applied the same approach (calling `DwmExtendFrameIntoClientArea` with a 1px top margin) in a Tauri / tao application, and the result looks very good.

<img width="654" height="436" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9bdbe3ab-42e3-499a-b872-d800f0fdd7b6" />

Here is a minimal reproduction using pure tao:

```rust
use tao::{
    dpi::LogicalSize,
    event::{Event, WindowEvent},
    event_loop::{ControlFlow, EventLoop},
    window::WindowBuilder,
};

#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
fn apply_win10_top_border_fix(window: &tao::window::Window) {
    use raw_window_handle::{HasWindowHandle, RawWindowHandle};
    use windows::Win32::Foundation::HWND;

    let hwnd = match window.window_handle() {
        Ok(handle) => match handle.as_raw() {
            RawWindowHandle::Win32(h) => HWND(h.hwnd.get() as *mut _),
            _ => return,
        },
        Err(_) => return,
    };

    unsafe {
        use windows::Win32::{
            Graphics::Dwm::DwmExtendFrameIntoClientArea,
            Graphics::Gdi::{
                BLACK_BRUSH, GetStockObject, RDW_ERASE, RDW_INVALIDATE, RDW_UPDATENOW, RedrawWindow,
            },
            UI::{
                Controls::MARGINS,
                WindowsAndMessaging::{GCLP_HBRBACKGROUND, SetClassLongPtrW},
            },
        };

        let black_brush = GetStockObject(BLACK_BRUSH);
        let _ = SetClassLongPtrW(hwnd, GCLP_HBRBACKGROUND, black_brush.0 as isize);
        let margins = MARGINS {
            cxLeftWidth: 0,
            cxRightWidth: 0,
            cyTopHeight: 1,
            cyBottomHeight: 0,
        };
        let _ = DwmExtendFrameIntoClientArea(hwnd, &margins);
        let _ = RedrawWindow(hwnd, None, None, RDW_INVALIDATE | RDW_ERASE | RDW_UPDATENOW);
    }
}

fn main() {
    let event_loop = EventLoop::new();

    let window = WindowBuilder::new()
        .with_title("Win10 Top Border Fix")
        .with_inner_size(LogicalSize::new(900.0, 600.0))
        .with_decorations(false) // Borderless / custom title bar
        .with_transparent(true) // Set to true if you want a transparent window
        .build(&event_loop)
        .expect("Failed to create window");

    // Apply the Windows 10 top border fix
    #[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
    apply_win10_top_border_fix(&window);

    event_loop.run(move |event, _, control_flow| {
        *control_flow = ControlFlow::Wait;

        if let Event::WindowEvent {
            event: WindowEvent::CloseRequested,
            ..
        } = event
        {
            *control_flow = ControlFlow::Exit;
        }
    });
}
```
Would it be possible to properly integrate a similar fix into tao (like WinUI 3 did) so that borderless windows on Windows 10 correctly display the top border by default?

