**Feature request**

Graph-capture support for training: a whole step (forward, backward, optimizer) recorded
once by `burn::tensor::capture` and replayed. Small models on CUDA are host-bound, and a
replay turns hundreds of launches into one dispatch. On a two-layer MLP, fwd+bwd and the
full step replay ×6–10 faster than eager on an RTX 2060.

Most of this already works. Autodiff's forward and backward replay bit-exactly, and so does
the whole step with **plain SGD at a constant learning rate**. With any other optimizer
setup, the capture still succeeds (`is_hardware() == true`), but the replays train on wrong
values, and nothing reports it. There are two causes, both in the optimizers.

1. **Host scalars are frozen into the graph.** On sm_70+ cubecl passes a kernel's scalars
   as grid constants in its parameter block
   ([`server.rs#L866`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/cubecl/blob/fc902af1cc662195ce9c8cc90d25ea048d222d54/crates/cubecl-cuda/src/compute/server.rs#L866),
   enabled at
   [`runtime.rs#L223`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/cubecl/blob/fc902af1cc662195ce9c8cc90d25ea048d222d54/crates/cubecl-cuda/src/runtime.rs#L223)),
   and a graph captures them by value. (Below sm_70 they would go through the info buffer
   instead; not tested here.) Two optimizer scalars change per step:
   - the learning rate, in every optimizer's `grad.mul_scalar(lr)`
     ([`adam.rs#L82`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/adam.rs#L82)),
     so any schedule stops at its value at capture time;
   - Adam(W)'s bias correction, a host `f32` computed from the host step count `time`
     ([`adam.rs#L194`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/adam.rs#L194)),
     so every replay applies the correction for the recorded step.
2. **Where an update lands is left to chance.** A replay reads the buffers the recording
   read and writes the buffers it wrote. So state advances across replays only if the
   update landed in the same buffer it read. `Optimizer::step` is functional: it returns
   new tensors. burn-cubecl's binary ops reuse an operand's buffer when no other tensor
   holds it (`can_mut`, lhs first), so whether a result reuses the state's buffer depends
   on reference counts and operand order.
   - Plain SGD's `tensor - delta`
     ([`sgd.rs#L94`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/sgd.rs#L94))
     and Adam's `moment_1.mul_scalar(β₁).add(…)`
     ([`adam.rs#L156`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/adam.rs#L156))
     have the state as an unshared lhs, so both land in place.
   - Momentum computes `grad.clone().mul_scalar(1 − d).add(velocity · m)`
     ([`momentum.rs#L64`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/momentum.rs#L64)).
     The lhs is a gradient temporary, so the new velocity lands in a fresh buffer each
     step. Replays keep reading the velocity from before the capture. The same ops with
     the velocity written back into its own buffer (the learning rate still a host
     scalar) replay bit-exactly.
   - AdamW computes `tensor.clone().mul_scalar(1 − lr·λ)`
     ([`adamw.rs#L97`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/adamw.rs#L97)).
     The clone makes the parameter shared, so the updated parameter moves to a fresh
     buffer every step. Every replay then recomputes the same step from the parameters
     as they were at capture: the loss below never moves.

   User code cannot repair this from outside. The per-parameter state lives in
   `ModuleOptimizer::param_context`, which is private
   ([`module_optimizer.rs#L61`](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/9147c11a19a5e2d46694c54d441eee0e006e096c/crates/burn-optim/src/optim/module/module_optimizer.rs#L61)),
   and its only accessor, `to_record`, reads to the host, which is illegal inside a
   capture.

Both are fixable within burn's public `Optimizer` trait. An AdamW that
(a) keeps the learning rate and the step count as `[1]` **device** tensors, and
(b) writes the parameter and every state tensor back into its own buffer
(`slice_assign`), replays bit-exactly, including under a decaying learning rate (sketch
under Additional context). PyTorch's answer to the same problem is `capturable=True` on
Adam/AdamW: the step count stays on the device and the learning rate may be a tensor.

**To Reproduce**

`Cargo.toml` (burn `9147c11a`, main 2026-09-22; the output below is identical on burn
`8f08fdad` / cubecl `fc902af1`):

```toml
