LBFGS includes every floating-point parameter in its flat parameter vector, but omits parameters without gradients from its flat gradient vector. This can panic or silently update frozen parameters.

Reproduced on Flex at `df0812e7dc2b1da8aaf8bb5f120e874039732567`, with both `LineSearchFn::None` and `StrongWolfe`:

```rust
use burn_core::module::{Module, Param};
use burn_core::tensor::{Device, Tensor};
use burn_optim::{GradientsParams, LBFGSConfig};

fn main() {
    let device = Device::default().autodiff(); // Enable burn-core/flex.
    let module = vec![
        Param::from_tensor(Tensor::<1>::from_floats([10.0], &device)).freeze(),
        Param::from_tensor(Tensor::<1>::from_floats([20.0], &device)),
    ];
    let mut optimizer = LBFGSConfig::new().with_max_iter(1).init();
    let (module, _) = optimizer.step(1.0, module, |module| {
        let loss = module[1].val().powf_scalar(2.0).sum();
        let grads = GradientsParams::from_grads(loss.backward(), &module);
        (loss.into_scalar::<f64>(), grads)
    });

    assert_eq!(module[0].val().into_scalar::<f32>(), 10.0); // Fails: 9.0.
    assert_eq!(module[1].val().into_scalar::<f32>(), 19.0);
}
```

Expected values: `[10, 19]`. Actual values: `[9, 19]`, because the one-element direction broadcasts to both parameters. With three scalar parameters and one missing gradient, addition instead panics on shapes `[3]` and `[2]`. Frozen and unused parameters both reproduce the problem, at any visit position.

Missing gradients need parameter-sized zero placeholders. Search directions also need to preserve those zero positions: saved L-BFGS history can otherwise move parameters that stop receiving gradients.

Originally tracked in https://github.com/Mikyx-1/burn/issues/165.

